      "rev_n_1_5e01ab_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_5e01ab_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_5e01ab",
        "thought_text": "Construct the lexicographically smallest permutation of s (sort s ascending) and the lexicographically largest permutation of t (sort t descending), then compare them directly. If smallest_s < largest_t, output Yes; otherwise No.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507267.7383087
      },
      "rev_n_2_93205d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_93205d_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_93205d",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible permutations of s and t (feasible given length <= 100? No, too large). Instead, use a greedy approach: try to make s' as small as possible and t' as large as possible by sorting, which is equivalent to the first strategy but framed as an optimization problem.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507267.7383237
      },
      "rev_n_3_9e450d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_9e450d_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_9e450d",
        "thought_text": "Analyze character counts. If the smallest character in s is greater than the largest character in t, it's impossible. Otherwise, construct specific strings to test the boundary condition where prefixes match and the first differing character decides the order.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507267.7383318
      },
      "rev_n_4_ed7b86_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_ed7b86_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_ed7b86",
        "thought_text": "Construct the lexicographically smallest permutation of s (sort s ascending) and the lexicographically largest permutation of t (sort t descending), then compare them directly. If smallest_s < largest_t, output Yes; otherwise No.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8705089256406217014",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507291.5674956
      },
      "rev_n_5_b70750_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_b70750_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_b70750",
        "thought_text": "Construct the lexicographically smallest permutation of s and the lexicographically smallest permutation of t, then check if smallest_s < smallest_t. If true, output Yes. If false, check if smallest_s == smallest_t; if they are equal, output No because we cannot make s' strictly smaller than t' if their minimal forms are identical.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8705089256406217014",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507291.5675116
      },
      "rev_n_6_4fd7c7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_4fd7c7_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_4fd7c7",
        "thought_text": "Construct the lexicographically largest permutation of s and the lexicographically largest permutation of t, then check if largest_s < largest_t. If true, output Yes. If false, check if largest_s == largest_t; if they are equal, output No.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8705089256406217014",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507291.5675209
      },
      "rev_n_5_b70750_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_b70750_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_b70750",
        "thought_text": "To correctly determine if $s' < t'$ is possible, we should construct the lexicographically smallest permutation of $s$ and the lexicographically largest permutation of $t$, then check if the former is strictly smaller than the latter; if this condition holds, output \"Yes\", otherwise \"No\".",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8705089256406217014",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_5_b70750_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_9a79928c1a",
        "created_at": 1794507307.8525968
      },
      "rev_n_7_60b7d0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_60b7d0_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_60b7d0",
        "thought_text": "Sort s in ascending order to make s' the lexicographically smallest possible string, and sort t in ascending order to make t' the lexicographically smallest possible string. If s' < t' holds, output Yes; otherwise, output No. This works because if the smallest s' is not less than the smallest t', no other arrangement of s can be less than any arrangement of t.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "20586868870505010",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507318.1487813
      },
      "rev_n_8_2d4e1e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_2d4e1e_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_2d4e1e",
        "thought_text": "Sort s in ascending order to minimize s' and sort t in descending order to maximize t'. Compare the resulting s' and t'. If s' < t', then it is possible; otherwise, it is impossible. Maximizing t' provides the easiest condition for s' to be smaller, so if this fails, no solution exists.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "20586868870505010",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507318.1487982
      },
      "rev_n_9_ea69ea_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_ea69ea_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_ea69ea",
        "thought_text": "Construct s' by sorting characters of s ascendingly and t' by sorting characters of t ascendingly. Then perform a direct lexicographical comparison. If they differ at the first mismatched index where s'[i] < t'[i], or if s' is a proper prefix of t', the answer is Yes. This is mathematically equivalent to the first strategy but explicitly details the comparison logic.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "20586868870505010",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507318.1488063
      },
      "rev_n_7_60b7d0_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_60b7d0_1",
        "node_id": "n_7_60b7d0",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (corrected): Sort s in ascending order to make s' the lexicographically smallest possible string, and sort t in descending order to make t' the lexicographically largest possible string. If s' < t' holds under these optimal arrangements, output Yes; otherwise, output No, because if the smallest s cannot be made smaller than the largest t, no other arrangement will satisfy the condition.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "20586868870505010",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_7_60b7d0_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_f7b1284829",
        "created_at": 1794507331.6382113
      },
